.TH DISK-JANITOR 1 "2026-11-06" "disk-janitor 0.3.0-lifecycle" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
disk-janitor \- submit local jobs with automatic scheduling and disk recovery
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B disk-janitor submit --
.I command
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B disk-janitor status
.RI [ job ]
.RB [ --json ]
.br
.B disk-janitor logs
.I job
.RB [ -f | --follow ]
.br
.B disk-janitor wait
.I job
.br
.B disk-janitor cancel
.I job
.br
.B disk-janitor
.RB { help | --help | -h | version }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B disk-janitor
accepts a command and returns a durable job ID. Deterministic machinery
chooses
resource estimates, queues work, admits fitting jobs concurrently, and
performs
authorized reclamation under capacity pressure. Agents submit and observe
jobs;
no resource estimates, owner labels, storage-root setup or restart options are
required in the public job interface.
.PP
Every submission is a new invocation, including identical commands. Acceptance
means queued work, not command completion. After a lost submission response,
inspect the queue before submitting again. Observing a job never starts or
replays its stored command.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B submit -- \fIcommand\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
Submit a new job and print its ID to standard output. The double dash is
required.
The command is executed directly, without an implicit shell. For pipelines,
redirections or multiple commands, explicitly submit a shell command.
The job retains the submitting environment, working directory and execution
permissions. Standard input is closed; stdout and stderr become retained job
logs.
The job can continue after the submitting client exits.
.TP
.B status \fR[\fIjob\fR] [\fB--json\fR]
Show one job or list durable public jobs. Without a job, text output also
reports
internal free space. JSON output is an object for one job and an array for the
queue. It includes identity, state, exit code, working directory, artifact
root
and executor information. Automatically managed foreground builds also appear.
Pressure-paused execution includes pressure_blocked, reason and blocked_since.
Historical legacy guard records are not listed.
.TP
.B logs \fIjob\fR [\fB-f\fR | \fB--follow\fR]
Print the retained diagnostic tail. With follow, stream updates until the job
is
terminal. At most the last 256 KiB is retained; expired output is unavailable.
Interrupting the observer does not cancel the job.
Foreground build wrappers preserve the caller's separate streams and do not
retain them; logs explains that mode instead of manufacturing missing output.
.TP
.B wait \fIjob\fR
Wait for a terminal outcome, print its ID and state, and return its exit
status.
There is no default waiting deadline. Interrupting wait leaves the job running
or queued.
.TP
.B cancel \fIjob\fR
Request cancellation and print the current state. This returns after
requesting
cancellation, not necessarily after teardown; use wait to observe its terminal
result. An agent may cancel only its own jobs. The local human user may
administer
jobs. Cancellation does not undo external effects.
.TP
.B help\fR, \fB--help\fR, \fB-h
Print a short command summary. Use
.B man disk-janitor
for this full manual.
.TP
.B version
Print the installed version.
.SH JOB STATES
Nonterminal states are
.BR queued ,
.B running
and
.BR cancelling .
Terminal states are
.BR succeeded ,
.BR failed ,
.B cancelled
and
.BR unknown .
.PP
Ordinary public jobs are nonrestartable. A surviving owned executor may
complete
after a frontend or guardian failure. The original worker may replace a failed
prelaunch guardian only when execution was not committed and no live execution
lease or result exists. Matching durable command completion evidence can resolve
a previously unknown outcome without another launch. If execution outcome
proof is lost, the
job settles as unknown; it is never automatically replayed or reported
successful.
There is no exactly-once guarantee for arbitrary external effects.
.SH DEFAULTS
Policy is automatic. Current per-command disk and memory estimates, in GiB,
are:
.TP
.B true, false, echo, printf
0.05 disk; 0.125 memory.
.TP
.B pytest, py.test, python* -m unittest
1 disk; 2 memory.
.TP
.B cargo, gradle, gradlew, xcodebuild, swift, swiftc, rustc
4 disk; 8 memory.
.TP
.B Other commands, including shell workflows
2 disk; 2 memory.
.PP
Private hashed command/workspace observations can raise future estimates.
Ordinary command budgets are not reduced merely because managed artifacts are
small; scripts may write elsewhere. These are estimates, not kernel-enforced
write quotas and are not held against other jobs.
.PP
A job waits only for a free slot. There is one slot per four cores and per
16 GiB of memory, whichever is fewer; MAX_RUNNING in the config file overrides
it. Waiting is first come, first served, and status shows the position. Slots
and queue places are kernel locks, so a crashed job or stale record holds
neither. Commands that plainly run no build tool take no slot. A job still
waiting after an hour fails with that reason.
.PP
Disk is decided once the slot is held. With the floor free (FLOOR_GIB, default
5) the job starts. Below it one recovery pass of a few seconds runs, then the
job starts or fails stating the free space, the need and what was recovered.
An external working-directory mount or an explicit /Volumes path argument is a
required mount with a 1 GiB floor; a missing one fails the job at once.
Other jobs' records, retention inventory, a running recovery and busy locks
never delay a start; what was ignored is logged in admission-notices.log.
Scripts' hidden output paths cannot be inferred automatically.
.PP
A watchdog runs every minute. Below the low-water mark (LOW_WATER_GIB, default
30) it recovers idle caches and the Gradle build outputs of idle git checkouts.
It retires reservation records nobody owns and starts the oldest waiter if one
is left beside a free slot.
A controller outside the owned command group samples required filesystem
reserves. Supported Linux systems can pause that group, request authorized
reclamation and resume the same processes after capacity returns; parent-death
continuation prevents a lost controller from stranding stopped work. Darwin
observes pressure without stopping arbitrary nonrestartable jobs. Status
distinguishes pressure observation and blocking. Sampling latency, escaped
writers and unrelated applications prevent
a hard quota guarantee. Nonrestartable jobs are never automatically replayed
or terminated merely to manufacture progress. Loss of all safe live control
evidence can prevent recovery; persisted PIDs alone never authorize signalling.
.SH OUTPUT AND SAFETY
The job environment contains
.B DISK_JANITOR_ARTIFACT_ROOT
naming its private managed artifact directory. Keep unique outputs there or in
appropriate source-owned locations. Unique artifacts are retained; log-tail
rotation does not delete them. Keep workflow children in the managed
foreground
process group rather than launching escaped writers.
.PP
Automatic pressure recovery covers inactive Cargo incremental output and proven
generated dependency objects in shared Cargo profiles, Gradle task/transform
build caches and Xcode DerivedData. Exported binaries, root libraries and
unmarked locations are preserved. Source, uncommitted
work,
credentials, databases, active outputs, disk images and unique artifacts
remain
protected. No manually approved path list is required for these categories.
.PP
The installer adds reversible PATH blocks for new zsh and bash shells and
scoped build-tool wrappers. Noninteractive cargo, Gradle, Xcode, Swift,
package-manager,
Make, CMake, Ninja and test-tool invocations then use synchronous managed jobs
with original arguments, stdin, separate output streams and exit status.
A nested tool joins only its actual live managed process group and held lease.
Terminal invocations pass directly to the original executable, preserving its
controlling terminal and job control without claiming managed execution.
Explicit executable paths, existing shells with an older PATH, interpreter
entrypoints and environments replacing PATH remain outside executable lookup;
submit those workflows explicitly. No shell trap or interpreter override is
installed.
.PP
RunnerPools needs the reviewed host broker bridge at its resource-lifetime
boundary. Public submission rejects unverified self-hosted RunnerPools contexts
rather than making a nested reservation. Platform sandbox permissions,
approvals
and explicit denials continue to apply. The runtime is not a privileged daemon
or a security boundary against other processes using the same OS account.
.SH EXIT STATUS
For submit, status, logs and cancel, zero means that the requested CLI
operation
succeeded; it does not imply that a submitted job succeeded.
.TP
.B 0
Successful CLI operation, or successful command when returned by wait.
.TP
.B 1
Job lookup, ownership, submission or other operational error.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid public-job command syntax or arguments.
.TP
.B 125
Wait could not establish a trustworthy execution outcome.
.TP
.B 126, 127
Wait reports that the command could not be executed, or was not found,
respectively.
.TP
.B 130
The observing CLI was interrupted with SIGINT. This does not cancel its job.
.TP
.B 143
Wait reports cancellation or a command terminated by SIGTERM.
.PP
Otherwise wait returns the command's exit status; signal termination is
normalized
to 128 plus the signal number. Command statuses can coincide with the codes
above.
The short-summary dispatcher returns 1 for an unknown top-level command.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISK_JANITOR_HOME
Application directory; defaults to $HOME/.disk-janitor. Normally leave this
unset.
.TP
.B DISK_JANITOR_ARTIFACT_ROOT
Set by the runtime for the submitted command; do not supply an output-root
option.
.TP
.B CODEX_THREAD_ID, CLAUDE_SESSION_ID
Agent identity used to restrict cancellation to owned jobs.
.TP
.B RUNNER_ENVIRONMENT, RUNNERPOOL_PROFILE
Declared self-hosted RunnerPools contexts are rejected by public submission.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.disk-janitor/jobs/JOB/
Private durable job metadata, lifetime locks, diagnostic tail and retained
artifacts.
Observe these through the CLI; do not edit records or remove locks.
.TP
.B ~/.disk-janitor/config.env
Existing shared-backend configuration. Ordinary jobs need no configuration.
.TP
.B ~/.disk-janitor/guard-reservations/
Shared-backend admission and result evidence, including legacy callers.
.SH EXAMPLES
Submit a test command, wait for its outcome, and read its retained output:
.PP
.nf
job=$(disk-janitor submit -- python3 -m unittest discover -s tests)
disk-janitor status "$job"
disk-janitor wait "$job"
disk-janitor logs "$job"
.fi
.PP
Submit an explicit shell workflow and follow its output:
.PP
.nf
job=$(disk-janitor submit -- sh -eu -c 'make build && make test')
disk-janitor logs "$job" -f
disk-janitor wait "$job"
.fi
.PP
Inspect the queue as JSON, or cancel an owned job and wait for teardown:
.PP
.nf
disk-janitor status --json
disk-janitor cancel "$job"
disk-janitor wait "$job"
.fi
.SH COMPATIBILITY
Existing install, uninstall, run, recover, reclaim, guard, admit, config and
guidance commands remain available for maintenance and legacy integrations.
In particular, run still means cleanup; it does not submit a command.
They are outside the ordinary public job interface documented here.
.SH VERIFICATION
The repository's formal/README.md documents bounded TLA+/TLC model checks,
assumptions and implementation-conformance tests. These are not an unbounded
proof of arbitrary commands, the OS or complete implementation refinement.
